WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.978
Your GPU is working flat out, and part of that

00:02.978 --> 00:06.409
work is on frames your robot has already thrown away.

00:07.209 --> 00:11.419
A robot camera gives you a frame every thirty-three milliseconds.

00:11.419 --> 00:13.233
Your detector needs fifteen.

00:13.233 --> 00:16.536
That fits, until something else wants the same GPU.

00:16.536 --> 00:20.227
Now the detector waits behind a block it cannot interrupt

00:20.227 --> 00:24.372
and by the time its answer arrives, the robot has already moved.

00:25.072 --> 00:27.663
Vigilant sits in front of your inference

00:27.663 --> 00:30.967
server and asks one question before every dispatch:

00:30.967 --> 00:34.400
will this result still be useful when it is finished?

00:34.400 --> 00:38.027
Work that cannot be is dropped before it costs anything.

00:38.027 --> 00:39.776
Same protocol, same models.

00:39.776 --> 00:42.432
In your client, only the address changes.

00:43.132 --> 00:47.185
Four decisions, all made before a request reaches the GPU.

00:47.185 --> 00:51.238
A newer frame replaces an older one that is still waiting.

00:51.238 --> 00:54.662
Work that would finish too late is never started.

00:54.662 --> 00:58.506
A long background job waits when protected work is due.

00:58.506 --> 01:02.768
And when time runs short, a smaller model variant takes over.

01:03.468 --> 01:07.795
It is built for machines where several models share one accelerator.

01:07.795 --> 01:11.867
A humanoid robot, whose vision must not wait behind its planner.

01:11.867 --> 01:14.540
Or driver assistance, with several cameras

01:14.540 --> 01:17.403
and a slower scene analysis on the same chip.

01:18.103 --> 01:20.843
Measured against a tuned Triton on the same GPU

01:20.843 --> 01:24.865
with a real detector and a background job that cannot be interrupted:

01:24.865 --> 01:27.372
the detector answers in time in ninety-nine

01:27.372 --> 01:30.229
percent of control cycles instead of eighty-five.

01:30.229 --> 01:32.152
Twenty times fewer missed cycles.

01:32.952 --> 01:35.194
And it is not tied to one machine.

01:35.194 --> 01:39.018
The same governor runs in front of Triton on an NVIDIA GPU

01:39.018 --> 01:43.896
and in front of TensorFlow Lite on the Adreno GPUs of two Android devices.

01:43.896 --> 01:48.907
On each of them, vig autotune measured the hardware and gave its own answer.

01:49.707 --> 01:52.067
You do not have to take our numbers.

01:52.067 --> 01:55.543
One command, vig autotune, measures your own machine:

01:55.543 --> 01:57.903
runtimes, concurrency, interference.

01:57.903 --> 02:02.165
It freezes a configuration and answers the question that matters:

02:02.165 --> 02:06.886
is the governor worth it here? On this laptop, above ninety percent load

02:06.886 --> 02:11.738
the protected stream goes from missing almost every cycle to missing none.

02:11.738 --> 02:15.148
And where you do not need it, it tells you that too.

02:15.948 --> 02:19.165
Point it at the inference server you already run.

02:19.165 --> 02:23.169
Same protocol, same models, and your client changes one line.

02:23.169 --> 02:27.305
Free for evaluation, and for up to three devices in production.

02:28.005 --> 02:30.605
Faster GPUs compute the past faster.

02:30.605 --> 02:32.628
Vigilant stops computing it.

02:32.628 --> 02:38.189
Run vig autotune on the machine you already have, and let the numbers decide.
